From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Chiou <chiouss@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 16:47:40 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] qualcommax: ipq50xx: add support for Zyxel NWA220AX-6E and
 WAX620D-6E

The Zyxel NWA220AX-6E is a ceiling-mount 802.11ax (Wi-Fi 6E) access
point based on the Qualcomm AP-MP03.1 reference design. A single QCN9024
radio can be switched between the 5 GHz and the 6 GHz band. The Zyxel
WAX620D-6E is electrically identical and differs only in the model ID
stored in the "mrd" partition, so both use the same image, which lists
the WAX620D-6E as an alternative model.

Hardware
--------
SoC:    Qualcomm IPQ5018
RAM:    1G DDR3
FLASH:  8M SPI-NOR (Winbond W25Q64DW)
        256M SPI-NAND (Winbond W25N02JW)
ETH:    1x 2.5 Gigabit Ethernet (QCA8081, PoE-in, "uplink")
        1x Gigabit Ethernet (IPQ5018 internal PHY, "lan1")
WiFi:   Qualcomm IPQ5018 (2.4GHz 802.11ax, 2x2)
        Qualcomm QCN9024 (5GHz or 6GHz 802.11ax, 4x4)
BT:     Qualcomm IPQ5018 (BT 5.1, not supported)
BTN:    1x Reset
LED:    1x Multi-Color (RGBW)
        2x port LEDs on each Ethernet port
Power:  802.3af/at PoE or 12V/2A DC

UART Console
------------
4-pin console header on the board.

Settings: 3.3V TTL, 115200 8N1

Installation Web-UI
-------------------
The device uses a dual-image setup and OpenWrt can only be installed as
image 0, as the bootloader always passes "ubi.mtd=rootfs" to the kernel.
When the currently running stock firmware is image 0, OpenWrt will be
installed as image 1, fail to boot and the device returns to stock
firmware. If this happens, install any version of stock firmware so
that it runs as image 1, before installing OpenWrt. Alternatively, if
there already is a valid stock firmware in image 1, the "debug
dual-image show" and "debug dual-image set boot-image image1" commands
can be used in the stock CLI via serial/SSH/telnet to switch to image 1.

 * Rename the OpenWrt factory image to a shorter name, for example
   "openwrt.bin" (the stock firmware has a character limit)
 * In the web interface, go to "Maintenance" -> "File Manager" ->
   "Firmware Package" (or click the link next to "Firmware Version"
   under "Device Information" on the dashboard)
 * Under "Upload File" browse to the renamed OpenWrt factory image and
   click on "Upload"

The factory image is a UBI image wrapped in an outer FIT carrying the
model IDs of both devices ("76 e1" for NWA220AX-6E, "75 e1" for
WAX620D-6E), which the stock firmware checks before accepting it. The
stock firmware flashes the "ubi" section into the inactive image. On
first boot, zyxel-bootconfig marks the OpenWrt image as the selected one
so the bootloader keeps booting it.

Switch Boot Image
-----------------
OpenWrt to stock: "zyxel-bootconfig-ipq set image1"
Stock to OpenWrt: "debug dual-image set boot-image image0"
In the bootloader: "atgo 0" / "atgo 1"

Unbrick / Revert to Stock with the Boot Module
----------------------------------------------
 * Disconnect the device from power
 * Configure your machine to 192.168.1.103/24 and start a TFTP server
 * Put the stock firmware image into the TFTP server root and rename it
   to "ZLD-current"
 * Establish a serial connection to the device through the console port
 * Connect the device to power
 * When prompted, press a key to abort automatic boot and enter debug
   mode
 * Use the "atnz" command to flash the firmware image
 * Use the "atgo" command to boot from the newly flashed image

Flash layout
------------
The NAND partitions follow the layout reported by the stock kernel,
which differs from the flash_partition XML in the vendor SDK:
0:TRAINING, rootfs (image 0), rootfs_1 (image 1), rootfs_data, myzyxel
and logs. The stock rootfs_data partition is named zyxel_rootfs_data to
not be picked up as the OpenWrt overlay.

MAC addresses
-------------
uplink:     base MAC (mrd partition, offset 0xfff8), matches the label
lan1:       base MAC + 1
WiFi 2.4G:  base MAC + 2
WiFi 5/6G:  base MAC + 3

Ethernet
--------
Both ports use the NSS-DP/SSDK stack. The SSDK switches the uniphy to
SGMII+ when the QCA8081 links at 2.5G and back to SGMII below that.

LEDs
----
The uplink port LEDs are driven by the QCA8081 in hardware: green is on
at a 2.5G link, amber is on at a 10/100/1000 link, and both blink on
traffic while lit.
The lan1 port LEDs are driven by the IPQ5018 internal PHY, whose LED
signals are routed through GPIO46 (green) and GPIO30 (amber): green is
on at a 1G link, amber is on at a 10/100 link, and both blink on
traffic while lit.

WiFi
----
The board data (calibration variant "Zyxel-NWA220AX-6E") comes from
the ipq-wifi-zyxel_nwa220ax-6e package in the zyxel feed.

The QCN9024 is handled by the ath11k QCN9074 support and firmware. Its
board data covers both 5 GHz and 6 GHz, which needs the ath11k patch to
advertise HT/VHT on the 5 GHz band of a 5/6 GHz radio.
6 GHz requires a country code and WPA3-SAE. Most clients only scan the
6 GHz preferred scanning channels (PSC: 5, 21, 37, ...), so choose one
of them instead of relying on ACS.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Mark Chiou <chiouss@gmail.com>
